@essay rh="FRONT MATTER · RULES OF THE HOUSE"
# Rules of the house
Seven commitments that keep this companion honest. They're the difference between a novel that takes religion seriously and one that uses it as set dressing.
rec:
I · The text isn't the Empire | Every entry keeps them apart, because the engine of this novel is the gap between what a passage says and what an institution can make it do.
II · Contested stays contested | Where scholars and traditions genuinely disagree, the disagreement is printed and left standing. The Empire may settle a question. The companion doesn't.
III · No character is an allegory | A biblical parallel can light a character up, and it never maps onto her. Every parallel ends with where it breaks, and the break is usually where the character gets interesting.
IV · Belief has a cost and a teacher | Nobody in this book believes anything for free. Someone taught it to them, and something's at stake in keeping it. Both go in the dossier.
V · Discovery outranks plan | If the research contradicts the outline, the research gets a hearing. The outline is a hypothesis, and this is where it gets tested.
VI · Nothing enters the novel untested | Every borrowed symbol, doctrine and parallel has to answer one question before it earns a scene.
VII · Possible isn't canon | Freewriting, inference and research notes stay marked until you approve them. A compelling idea stays possible until it survives the manuscript, the source files and your own decision.
pull: "Does this serve Thy Kingdom Come, or does it only prove I did the reading?"
side:
k: The test
stext: Every page in this book answers to the question at the foot of this one.
marg
/end

@page rh="FRONT MATTER · BEFORE YOU BEGIN"
# Before you begin
Answer these once, in ink, before you read a single entry, and don't go back and tidy the answers later. The point is a record of where you started, so that in a year you can see what the reading did to you and how much of it belongs to the novel.
